{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["The meaning of <b>עֵקֶב</b> (\"eikev\") is like בַּעֲבוּר (\"because of\"), as in עֵקֶב אֲשֶׁר שָׁמַע אַבְרָהָם בְּקֹלִי (Genesis 26:5). Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 7:12) wrote: \"If you heed the minor commandments that a person tramples under his heels, HaShem will keep His promise to you.\" And Scripture mentioned \"the statutes\" — perhaps it warns concerning the minor statutes, such as the laws of monetary matters, that one should not despise them. And the commentators said that the meaning of <b>עֵקֶב</b> is reward at the end, as in בְּשָׁמְרָם עֵקֶב רָב (Psalms 19:12); it would mean: \"And it shall be, in the end, that you heed the statutes and keep them, HaShem will keep for you the covenant and the kindness, and love you.\" And this is correct, for in the holy language they call the beginning of anything <b>רֹאשׁ</b> (\"head\"), as in רֹאשׁ דְּבָרְךָ אֱמֶת (Psalms 119:160), and likewise גְּדוֹל הַדּוֹר רֹאשׁ הָעָם (Numbers 25:4), and the choice <b>רֹאשׁ בְּשָׂמִים</b> (Song of Songs 4:14). And so they call the end of anything <b>עֵקֶב</b>, for the language grasps its metaphor from a person — the head is first and the heel is last and final. And so Scripture says (below 28:13, 44) לְרֹאשׁ וּלְזָנָב, by way of the metaphor of an animal's body. And Onkelos (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 7:12) translated it חֲלָף, like חֵלֶף עֲבֹדַתְכֶם (Numbers 18:31); he made it an expression of circuitousness, derived from וְהָיָה הֶעָקֹב לְמִישׁוֹר (Isaiah 40:4) — the crooked path that goes round and round; and so עֲקֻבָּה מִדָּם (Hosea 6:8) means surrounded and enclosed. It would mean: \"And the cause of your heeding the statutes and doing them shall be that HaShem keeps His covenant for you.\" And he explained well. And similar to this is בִּגְלַל הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה (Deuteronomy 15:10) — בְּסִבָּתוֹ, from the language of וְגָלְלוּ אֶת הָאֶבֶן (Genesis 29:3). And in my opinion, every expression of עֲקִיבָה is rolling and circuitousness: עָקוֹב הַלֵּב (Jeremiah 17:9), וַיַּעְקְבֵנִי זֶה פַעֲמַיִם (Genesis 27:36), וְיֵהוּא עָשָׂה בְּעָקְבָּה (II Kings 10:19) — all are matters of turnings and circuits. Therefore they call Jacob <b>יְשֻׁרוּן</b>, for it is the opposite of עָקֹב — straightness. And likewise the back of the foot, which is called עֵקֶב, as in וְיָדוֹ אֹחֶזֶת בַּעֲקֵב עֵשָׂו (Genesis 25:26), is called so because it is rounded, just as the language calls the palm of the hand and the foot <b>כַּפּוֹת</b> because they are like golden bowls. And this is customary in the language, as they said in Sifrei (Berakhot 2): מִימִינוֹ אֵשׁ דָּת לָמוֹ (Deuteronomy 33:2) — when the utterance went forth from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, it would go from His right, as it were, to the left of Israel, and would circuit the camp of Israel twelve mil by twelve mil — meaning, surround it. And so their language (Bava Kamma 113a): בָּאִים עָלָיו בַּעֲקִיפִין — with turnings and rollings, like עֲקִיבִין, for these two letters are equivalent to them, as I have already explained (Leviticus 19:20). And Scripture mentioned these statutes to warn greatly concerning the statutes, for a numerous people will not all be careful in all the commandments so as not to sin in them at all; rather, through the statutes they will uphold the Torah, as is said of them (Deuteronomy 21:21) וְכָל יִשְׂרָאֵל יִשְׁמְעוּ וְיִרָאוּ. And furthermore, many will take pity and be reluctant to stone the man or burn him after the sin has been done, as it says (Deuteronomy 19:13) לֹא תָחוֹס עֵינְךָ. And furthermore, that they may fear the powerful and the intimidators, as He said (Deuteronomy 1:17) לֹא תָגוּרוּ מִפְּנֵי אִישׁ כִּי הַמִּשְׁפָּט לֵאלֹהִים הוּא, and He said concerning the false prophet (Deuteronomy 18:22) לֹא תָגוּר מִמֶּנּוּ. And He mentioned all of these concerning the inciter (Deuteronomy 13:9): לֹא תֹאבֶה לוֹ וְלֹא תִשְׁמַע אֵלָיו וְלֹא תָחוֹס עֵינְךָ עָלָיו וְלֹא תַחְמֹל וְלֹא תְכַסֶּה עָלָיו. He warns לֹא תִשְׁמַע אֵלָיו on account of his intimidation, and לֹא תָחוֹס עֵינְךָ on account of the mercy that the faint of heart will feel for those sentenced, and לֹא תְכַסֶּה — that you should not remain silent because of his power and the fear of his family members."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:26.210874588Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}